   "early_warning": "Covenant reached around 2027-03-14; only $4.0M of the round committed at 2026-10-31"
  }
 ],
 "consensus": {
  "consensus": "The company's pitch, and the way an ag-robotics Series A is usually framed, assumes: the only autonomous open-field strawberry picker above 90% pick accuracy; a $310M obtainable market off a $3.1B serviceable one; a 41% gross margin and a 2.1-season payback; $3.2M of ARR; and a $72M pre-money in line with what Monarch ($220M+ raised) and Carbon Robotics ($232M) have attracted.",
  "our_view": "The labour problem is real and the machine works, but the price is not supported: the market is 8.7x smaller than the headline, the unit economics are negative at the price actually sold, and the technology moat is a conditional licence with a 2027 cliff. The thesis is non-consensus on valuation only: invest at $48M-$55M pre-money, or $12M tranched with $6M released on 2027 milestones.",
  "evidence": "Section 3: the 94% is a share of detected ripe fruit on 212 company-chosen rows, excluding tunnels, standing water and slopes above 4%; independent trials report 74-87% and harvest-level recovery is ~75-80% [S53, S56, S42, S57]. Section 4: 50,000 acres / 3.5 x $16,500 caps the whole US strawberry robot-revenue pool at $235.7M, so $310M is 132% of the base [S53]. Section 6: after $12,000 of fleet depreciation the unit is -$8,100 a season at the 2026 price and -$650 at the renewal price with the target cost [S53]. Section 7: 56% of the $3.2M ARR is non-recurring or non-binding; 46% of FY2026E revenue is one related-party services contract that ends 2027-06-30 [S53]. Section 8: $72M pre is 27.7x FY2026E revenue and 51.4x contracted robot revenue [S53].",
  "proof_event": "The 2027 season: 360 robots in service with at least 250 contracted at $12,700 or more, a measured v4 unit cost at or below $33,000, and an independent harvest-level trial above 85% with bruising no worse than a hand-picked control."
 },
 "one_question": {
  "question": "What is the harvest-level recovery rate — marketable berries in the clamshell divided by ripe berries on the plant — and the bruise rate against a hand-picked control, measured on blocks Orrin did not select?",
  "why": "The 94% divides by detected ripe fruit and excludes tunnels, standing water and slopes above 4%, so it cannot tell an investor whether the machine supplements a crew or replaces one. If harvest-level recovery is 75-80% with 3.8% bruising, the price ceiling is a harvest-aid rental and the $310M market, the 360-robot plan and the $72M pre-money all fall together; if it is above 85% with bruising at or below hand-picked fruit, the same numbers become conservative and the 2027 plan is financeable. It is the one measurement that reprices every other assumption in the case."
 },
 "modules": {
  "analogs": "Advanced Farm Technologies (California, 2021-2025): raised a $25M Series B with Kubota and Yamaha Motor and a CNH investment, ran commercial strawberry and apple deployments with Driscoll's and California Giant, and ceased operations in July 2025 (S93, S114, S116, S117). Lesson: commercial deployments and OEM capital are not proof of a viable unit economic model in this crop. Abundant Robotics (2015-2021): raised about $12M, ran apple-harvesting trials under RaaS, and sold its IP in liquidation on 2021-06-29 (S91). Lesson: RaaS in fruit harvesting has a 12-month cash cycle and no margin for a missed season. Tortuga AgTech (2016-2025): raised a reported $47.7M in tabletop and greenhouse harvesting and sold its IP, assets and engineering team to Oishii on 2025-03-24 (S115, S118). Lesson: the best available outcome for a sub-scale harvesting-robot company is an asset sale to a strategic. Deere/Bear Flag Robotics (2021, $250M) and Deere/Blue River (2017, $305M): pre-scale autonomy businesses bought by an OEM with a dealer network (S96, S92). Lesson: a $150M-$300M OEM exit for a proven autonomy stack is plausible, and it is the ceiling this case should underwrite. AppHarvest/Root AI (2021, $60M) sets the harvesting-specific clearing price far below the autonomy comparables (S91, S93).",
  "capital_efficiency": "$38.5M of equity, notes and grants raised since 2022 for $2.6M of FY2026E revenue and $0.71M of RaaS revenue: $0.24 of cumulative revenue per equity dollar, falling to $0.15 on FY2027E (S53, derived). Burn multiple of 7.0x in FY2026 ($4.9M of burn over $0.7M of net new revenue) and 5.9x in FY2027, against a 3x benchmark. Fleet replacement cost of $3.07M (64 robots at $48,000) against a $90M post-money. The $18M buys one 2027 season: $9.9M of robots, $7.1M of cash operating loss and $1-2M of working capital leave $1.5-3.5M at end-2027; at the $12M minimum close there is a $1.6M shortfall before working capital. Capital efficiency is the weakest dimension: the model needs price up 74%, unit cost down 35% and service cost down 44% at the same time, while 360 robots require ~45 technicians against nine field staff (S53).",
  "position_sizing": "Meridian's $12M at $72M pre-money buys 13.33% of a $90M post-money. Public-equity sizing rules do not apply to a pre-revenue-scale private company, so the check size is set by the downside and the follow-on reserve: the maximum loss is the full $12M, and the base case needs a further $6-10M of Series B money in 2028 to protect the stake. Recommendation: commit $6M now at a $48M-$55M pre-money, with $6M released on 2027 milestones (250 robots contracted at $12,700 or more, measured v4 unit cost at or below $33,000, an independent harvest-level trial above 85%, and the Calder licence confirmed exclusive), and reserve $6M for the Series B. At $50M pre-money the same $12M buys 17.65% and returns 3.68x at a $250M exit against 2.78x at $72M. A $12M cheque at $72M with no tranching and no protective provisions, with Tallis holding an observer seat and five commercial roles, is not an investable structure for a lead."
 },
