{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"research_plan (by Lead Analyst):
{
 "spines": [
  {
   "spine": "Growth-stage startup: unit economics, GTM repeatability, retention, margin, financing need",
   "must_answer": "Can growth scale without destroying economics: does the $9,500 per robot-season RaaS contract earn a real margin once fleet depreciation, field service, transport, insurance and utilization across a 7-month California or 3-month Florida season are fully loaded, and can Orrin go from 75 paid robot-seasons in 2026 to 360 robots in 2027 without a capital or service-quality break?"
  },
  {
   "spine": "Deep tech / hard tech: technical feasibility, validation evidence, manufacturing, capex, regulation",
   "must_answer": "What has been proven, under what conditions, and what remains unproven: is the 94% pick rate, 3.8% bruising and 86% uptime credible outside the 212 rows Orrin's own field team selected, and is the IP defensible given the Calder exclusive licence, the rejected gripper claim and the unfiled PCT national-phase deadline?"
  },
  {
   "spine": "Industrial / hardware: BOM, supply chain, quality, installation/service economics, utilization",
   "must_answer": "Can the company produce and support at scale: can Orrin cut unit cost from $48,000 to $31,000-$33,000 through a contract manufacturer that is also a noteholder, customer, v4 manufacturer and board observer, and support 360 robots across California and Florida with 9 field staff plus a related-party service contractor without warranty, bruising or utilization problems?"
  }
 ],
 "industry_map": "Agricultural robotics for specialty crops. Value chain: genetics and inputs -> growers (about 50,000 US strawberry acres, concentrated in California's Central Coast and Florida's Plant City) -> harvest labor (hand crews, H-2A guest workers, grower housing) -> harvest aids (carts, conveyors, picker-assist platforms) -> autonomous harvest robots -> coolers/packers/shippers -> retailers. Decisive bottleneck: the harvest-window labor gap and the per-tray picking cost; a robot must beat hand-pick economics across a 7-month California season with bruising below roughly 5% and uptime above 90%, or growers will not convert pilots to paid seasons. Orrin's place: a turnkey Robots-as-a-Service provider that owns, insures and maintains a 64-robot fleet, sells per robot-season at $9,500 (2026) rising to $16,500 list (2027), depends on Tallis Agricultural Machinery for v4 contract manufacturing and on an exclusive Calder University licence for core perception IP.",
 "geo_lens": "Material. Map four geographies: (1) revenue market — California Central Coast (Salinas, Watsonville, Santa Maria) and Florida (Plant City), where season length (about 7 months vs 3-4) changes robot utilization and payback; (2) manufacturing/supplier market — Guadalajara, Mexico (Orrin Fieldworks Mexico, 7 staff) and Tallis's v4 build, exposed to USMCA and cross-border logistics; (3) competitor and comparable market — US, European greenhouse/tabletop robotics and Israeli/US field robotics; (4) capital and exit market — US venture capital and strategic acquirers among agricultural equipment OEMs. The California/Florida labor-cost and immigration-policy difference drives adoption and pricing power.",
 "policy_lens": "Material. The value proposition is a function of US farm-labor policy: H-2A guest-worker availability and wage rates, California wage orders and overtime rules, and immigration enforcement all set the hand-pick cost the robot must beat. US trade policy toward Mexican assembly (USMCA review, tariff risk) sets part of Orrin's cost base. USDA SBIR grants ($650,000 Phase II, 2024-2026) are a non-dilutive funding line that ends in 2026. Policy does not create Orrin's moat; it sets the speed of customer adoption and a cost-side risk.",
 "regulatory_databases": [
  "USPTO Patent Center and PatentsView (patent applications US 17/902,118, 18/655,430, 19/204,876, 63/811,204; PCT/US2025/027761 national phase)",
  "Delaware Division of Corporations (Orrin Fieldworks, Inc. incorporation and good standing)",
  "California Secretary of State business registry (Orrin Fleet Services LLC)",
  "USDA SBIR award database (Phase II grant $650,000, 2024-2026)",
  "USDA National Agricultural Statistics Service (strawberry acreage, California and Florida)",
  "California Department of Food and Agriculture and California Strawberry Commission (acreage, grower concentration, harvest labor)",
  "SEC EDGAR (comparable public ag-equipment and robotics filings, for valuation and exit comps)",
  "US Department of Labor OFLC (H-2A program data and adverse effect wage rates)"
 ],
 "gates": [
  "Confirm company identity, legal entity, geography, stage, and business model.",
  "Choose 1-3 report spines from report_strategy_playbook.md.",
  "Decide whether the geographic market context lens is material; if yes, map home, revenue, expansion, supplier, comparable-company, capital, exit markets and the investor perspective.",
  "Decide whether the policy and geopolitics lens is material; if yes, map jurisdictions and perspective.",
  "Inventory the client documents."
 ],
 "hypothesis": "Wait for milestone. The problem is real and the RaaS model is the right shape for growers, but the evidence does not yet support leading an $18M Series A at a $72M pre-money: the 94% pick rate rests on 212 rows the company chose, 41% gross margin excludes fleet depreciation, 53% of 2026 paid robot-seasons sit with two named customers one of which employs the COO's former employer, and the plan needs $9.9M of the round to build robots whose cost is still a supplier quote. The 2027 season conversion (360 robots, $8M ARR, unit cost below $36,000, 95% pick rate at 3 farms) is the milestone that would change the view; a smaller check at a lower price or a tranched close tied to 2027 field data is the investable structure.",
 "author": "Lead Analyst",
 "saved_at": "2026-10-31T07:13:10+00:00"
}"}],"isError":false}

