   "confidence": "medium-low",
   "disproof": "A larger H-2A cap, an AEWR freeze, backpay under the 2026-08-26 ruling, or grower surveys showing crews available",
   "impact": "-15% to -20% value; adoption slows and the 2027 book prices lower",
   "early_warning": "DOL rulemaking on H-2A eligibility or wage methodology; the California all-in hand-pick cost falling year on year"
  }
 ],
 "consensus": {
  "consensus": "The pitch, and the way an ag-robotics Series A is usually framed, assumes the only autonomous open-field strawberry picker above 90% accuracy, a $310M obtainable market off a $3.1B serviceable one, a 41% gross margin and a 2.1-season payback, $3.2M of ARR and a $72M pre-money in line with large peer rounds such as Monarch's.",
  "our_view": "The labour problem is real and the machine sells seasons, but the price is not supported: the observable market is 8.7x smaller than the headline, unit contribution is negative at the price actually sold, and the technology moat is a conditional licence with a 2027 cliff. The thesis is non-consensus on valuation and on harvest-level performance, not on the existence of the problem. We would not lead at $72M; the investable structure is $48M-$55M pre-money with $6M tranched on 2027 milestones and a Kestrel standstill.",
  "evidence": "Section 3: 94% is a share of detected ripe fruit on 212 company-chosen rows, excluding tunnels, standing water and slopes above 4%; independent trials report 72%-87% and harvest-level recovery is about 75%-80% [S53, S56, S42, S57, S181]. Section 4: 50,000 acres / 3.5 x $16,500 caps the whole US strawberry robot-revenue pool at $235.7M, so $310M is 132% of the base [S53]. Section 6: after $12,000 of fleet depreciation the unit is -$8,100 a season at the 2026 price and -$650 at the renewal with the target cost [S53]. Section 7: 56% of the $3.2M ARR is non-recurring or non-binding; 46% of FY2026E revenue is one related-party services contract ending 2027-06-30 [S53]. Section 8: $72M pre is 27.7x FY2026E revenue and 51.4x contracted robot revenue [S53].",
  "proof_event": "An independent harvest-level trial above 85% with bruising no worse than a hand-picked control, 250 robots contracted for 2027 at $12,700 or more, a measured v4 unit cost at or below $33,000, and a Kestrel standstill signed before the money is wired."
 },
 "one_question": {
  "question": "What is the harvest-level recovery rate, marketable berries in the clamshell divided by ripe berries on the plant, and the bruise rate against a hand-picked control, measured on blocks Orrin did not select?",
  "why": "The 94% divides by detected ripe fruit and excludes tunnels, standing water and slopes above 4%, so it cannot tell an investor whether the machine supplements a crew or replaces one. At 75%-80% recovery with 3.8% bruising the price ceiling is a harvest-aid rental and the $310M market, the 360-robot plan and the $72M pre-money fall together; above 85% with bruising no worse than hand-picked fruit, the same numbers become conservative and the 2027 plan is financeable. It is the one measurement that reprices every other assumption in the case."
 },
 "modules": {
  "analogs": "",
  "capital_efficiency": "FY2026E revenue $2.6M against $38.5M of equity and notes raised, $0.24 of revenue per equity dollar (0.15 on FY2027E); burn multiple 7.0x FY2026 and 5.9x FY2027; $18M buys 300 robots at $33,000 but leaves $1.5-3.5M at end-2027.",
  "position_sizing": "Zero at $72M pre-money; $6M at $48M-$55M with $6M released on 2027 milestones (independent harvest trial above 85% with clean bruising, 250 robots contracted at $12,700 or more, measured v4 cost at or below $33,000, Calder licence confirmed exclusive after the 100-robot test). Maximum loss $12M; $6M reserved for a Series B. At $50M pre-money a $12M-only cheque buys 19.35% of the $62M post and returns 4.0x at a $250M exit; 17.65% and 3.68x need the full $18M."
