{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"scenario_analysis (by Lead Analyst):
{
 "scenarios": [
  {
   "case": "Bull",
   "probability": "12%",
   "driver": "The v4 robot hits $31,000-$33,000 at 300 units, 360 robots are delivered for the April 2027 season, at least 250 sell at the $16,500 list, an independent trial shows harvest-level recovery above 85% with bruising no worse than hand-picked fruit, and the Calder licence clears its 100-robot test on 2027-12-31.",
   "evidence": "64 robots and 75 paid seasons already delivered; 110 contracted and 50 LOIs for 2027; Tallis has quoted the v4 BOM (S53, unverified). Deere paid $250M-$305M for pre-scale autonomy businesses (S178, S92, S96).",
   "outcome": "$8M+ of ARR exiting 2027, an OEM partnership or Series B in 2028, and a trade sale at $300M-$400M in 2030-2031; Meridian returns 3.3x-4.4x before further dilution ($40M-$53M on $12M).",
   "implication": "This is the case the $72M price already assumes; only a tranche that captures it at a lower entry makes it an investment."
  },
  {
   "case": "Base",
   "probability": "38%",
   "driver": "The build lands late or short: 180-250 robots at the $12,700 renewal, $2-3M of RaaS in 2027, a flat or down Series B in 2028 once the $36.8M-$39.3M preference stack is visible, and an OEM trade sale at $150M-$250M in 2029-2031.",
   "evidence": "2027 is 44% covered (110 contracted plus 50 LOIs of 360); renewal is below the $13,350 break-even at current service cost; the $18M funds one season and leaves $1.5-3.5M at end-2027 (S53, derived). Agtech deal count fell about 20% in 2025 (S180), and the category base rate is failure or absorption (S91, S114, S115, S176).",
   "outcome": "$150M-$250M sale in 2029-2031; Meridian returns 1.7x-2.8x at $72M, or 1.3x-2.2x after a 20% Series B dilution. At $50M pre-money a $12M-only cheque (19.35% of a $62M post) returns 2.4x-4.0x; 2.2x-3.7x holds only if the full $18M closes.",
   "implication": "Reprice the entry or tranche; at $72M the base case is a thin venture return, not a loss."
  },
  {
   "case": "Bear",
   "probability": "50%",
   "driver": "One domino takes several symptoms: the round slips past Q1 2027, the v4 build misses the April 2027 season, the fleet stays below 100 robots, the Kestrel $1.5M minimum-cash covenant binds around 2027-03-14, the Calder licence becomes non-exclusive on 2027-12-31, a second grower leaves over bruising, and cheaper hand labour after the 2025-10 H-2A wage cut slows conversion.",
   "evidence": "$1.79M of usable cash at 2026-10-31, about 4.4 months to the covenant; only $4.0M of the $18M is committed and a $12M close is $1.63M short before working capital; $1.6M of secured debt ranks ahead of the preferred; the gripper patent claims are rejected and the PCT national phase was not entered; the IP sits in the issuer while the fleet and grower contracts sit in Orrin Fleet Services LLC (S53, S128, S130). The $19.97 AEWR governed through 2025-10-01; DOL 90 FR 47914 cut the operative California floor to $16.90 from 2026-01-01, and AB 2646 restores $19.75 from 2027-01-01 (S190, S195). The covenant rests on the deck alone: no loan agreement or UCC-1 is registered.",
   "outcome": "A secured-lender event or a distressed asset sale in 2027-2028; the entity split may prevent the licence and fleet from being sold together, so Meridian recovers $0-$12M (0-1.0x). If the facility has no minimum-cash test the failure is a 25%-40% down round rather than a secured-lender event: bear weight falls from 50% to about 35%, base rises to 53%, and the $48M-$55M structure becomes Invest with conditions.",
   "implication": "Do not lead without a Kestrel standstill signed with the term sheet, a licence assignment consent and a covenant waiver as closing conditions."
  }
 ],
 "assumptions": [
  {
   "assumption": "Harvest-level recovery reaches 85% or more, with bruising at or below a hand-picked control, on blocks Orrin did not select",
   "confidence": "medium-low",
   "disproof": "An independent trial below 80% of ripe fruit harvested, or a second paying grower leaving over fruit quality",
   "impact": "-40% enterprise value; the product becomes a harvest aid and the price ceiling falls",
   "early_warning": "Further pilot exits after Grower E in Plant City; retail or grower quality audits on machine-picked fruit"
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The v4 robot can be built at $31,000-$33,000 at 300 units (35% below the $48,000 v3 cost) by Tallis in Guadalajara",
   "confidence": "low",
   "disproof": "A contract-manufacturing quote above $36,000, or the first 20-unit build costing more than $40,000",
   "impact": "-25% to -35% enterprise value; the $12,700 renewal never clears break-even",
   "early_warning": "v4 design freeze slips past 2027-01; Tallis re-quotes the BOM; gripper cost-down abandoned"
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The 2027 book clears at $12,700 renewal or better, with 360 robots sold rather than 160",
   "confidence": "low",
   "disproof": "Renewals signed at $9,500-$12,700, or fewer than 250 robots contracted by 2027-02",
   "impact": "-30% value; the base case becomes a bridge round at a flat or down price",
   "early_warning": "110 contracted plus 50 LOIs unchanged through Q1 2027; a grower renegotiating after a bruising complaint"
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The round closes at $18M by Q1 2027 with a Kestrel standstill, so the company reaches a Series B without breaching the $1.5M minimum-cash covenant",
   "confidence": "low",
   "disproof": "A close after Q1 2027, a $16M rather than $18M raise, or a burn above $410,000 a month",
   "impact": "-100% of the equity in a secured-lender event; if no minimum-cash test exists, a 25%-40% down round instead",
   "early_warning": "Covenant reached around 2027-03-14; only $4.0M of the round committed at 2026-10-31; no loan agreement or UCC-1 in the register"
  },
  {
   "assumption": "The wage floor rebinds: AB 2646 restores $19.75 on 2027-01-01, and no H-2A expansion or AEWR freeze cuts the hand-pick cost advantage",
   "confidence": "medium-low",
   "disproof": "A larger H-2A cap, an AEWR freeze, backpay under the 2026-08-26 ruling, or grower surveys showing crews available",
   "impact": "-15% to -20% value; adoption slows and the 2027 book prices lower",
   "early_warning": "DOL rulemaking on H-2A eligibility or wage methodology; the California all-in hand-pick cost falling year on year"
  }
 ],
 "consensus": {
  "consensus": "The pitch, and the way an ag-robotics Series A is usually framed, assumes the only autonomous open-field strawberry picker above 90% accuracy, a $310M obtainable market off a $3.1B serviceable one, a 41% gross margin and a 2.1-season payback, $3.2M of ARR and a $72M pre-money in line with large peer rounds such as Monarch's.",
  "our_view": "The labour problem is real and the machine sells seasons, but the price is not supported: the observable market is $235.7M at 100% adoption, not the $310M headline, unit contribution is negative at the price actually sold, and the technology moat is a conditional licence with a 2027 cliff. The thesis is non-consensus on valuation and on harvest-level performance, not on the existence of the problem. We would not lead at $72M; the investable structure is $48M-$55M pre-money with $6M tranched on 2027 milestones and a Kestrel standstill.",
  "evidence": "Section 3: 94% is a share of detected ripe fruit on 212 company-chosen rows, excluding tunnels, standing water and slopes above 4%; independent trials report 72%-87% and harvest-level recovery is about 75%-80% [S53, S56, S42, S57, S181]. Section 4: 50,000 acres / 3.5 x $16,500 caps the whole US strawberry robot-revenue pool at $235.7M, so $310M is 132% of the base; the 15%-by-2031 pool is $35.4M at list [S53]. Section 6: after $12,000 of fleet depreciation the unit is -$8,100 a season at the 2026 price and -$650 at the renewal with the target cost [S53]. Section 7: 56% of the $3.2M ARR is non-recurring or non-binding; 46% of FY2026E revenue is one related-party services contract ending 2027-06-30 [S53]. Section 8: $72M pre is 27.7x FY2026E revenue and 51.4x contracted robot revenue, and section 12's probability-weighted exit value is $116.8M, 1.41x at the current price [S53].",
  "proof_event": "An independent harvest-level trial above 85% with bruising no worse than a hand-picked control, 250 robots contracted for 2027 at $12,700 or more, a measured v4 unit cost at or below $33,000, and a Kestrel standstill signed before the money is wired."
 },
 "one_question": {
  "question": "What is the harvest-level recovery rate, marketable berries in the clamshell divided by ripe berries on the plant, and the bruise rate against a hand-picked control, measured on blocks Orrin did not select?",
  "why": "The 94% divides by detected ripe fruit and excludes tunnels, standing water and slopes above 4%, so it cannot tell an investor whether the machine supplements a crew or replaces one. At 75%-80% recovery with 3.8% bruising the price ceiling is a harvest-aid rental and the $310M market, the 360-robot plan and the $72M pre-money fall together; above 85% with bruising no worse than hand-picked fruit, the same numbers become conservative and the 2027 plan is financeable. It is the one measurement that reprices every other assumption in the case."
 },
 "modules": {
